NNAAMMEE
       basename, dirname - parse pathname components

LLIIBBRRAARRYY
       Standard C library (_l_i_b_c, _-_l_c)

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       ##iinncclluuddee <<lliibbggeenn..hh>>

       cchhaarr **ddiirrnnaammee((cchhaarr **_p_a_t_h));;
       cchhaarr **bbaasseennaammee((cchhaarr **_p_a_t_h));;

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       Warning: there are two different functions bbaasseennaammee(); see below.

       The functions ddiirrnnaammee() and bbaasseennaammee() break a null‐terminated pathname
       string  into  directory  and  filename  components.  In the usual case,
       ddiirrnnaammee() returns the string up to, but not including, the  final  '/',
       and bbaasseennaammee() returns the component following the final '/'.  Trailing
       '/' characters are not counted as part of the pathname.

       If  _p_a_t_h  does  not  contain  a slash, ddiirrnnaammee() returns the string "."
       while bbaasseennaammee() returns a copy of _p_a_t_h.  If _p_a_t_h is  the  string  "/",
       then both ddiirrnnaammee() and bbaasseennaammee() return the string "/".  If _p_a_t_h is a
       null  pointer  or  points  to  an empty string, then both ddiirrnnaammee() and
       bbaasseennaammee() return the string ".".

       Concatenating the string returned by ddiirrnnaammee(), a "/", and  the  string
       returned by bbaasseennaammee() yields a complete pathname.

       Both  ddiirrnnaammee()  and  bbaasseennaammee() may modify the contents of _p_a_t_h, so it
       may be desirable to pass a copy when calling one of these functions.

       These functions may return  pointers  to  statically  allocated  memory
       which  may be overwritten by subsequent calls.  Alternatively, they may
       return a pointer to some part of _p_a_t_h, so that the string  referred  to
       by  _p_a_t_h  should not be modified or freed until the pointer returned by
       the function is no longer required.

       The following list of examples (taken from SUSv2) shows the strings re‐
       turned by ddiirrnnaammee() and bbaasseennaammee() for different paths:
              lb lb lb l l l  l.   path dirname   basename  /usr/lib  /usr lib
              /usr/     /    usr    usr  .    usr    /    /    /   .    .    .
              ..   .    ..

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEE
       Both  ddiirrnnaammee()  and  bbaasseennaammee()  return  pointers  to  null‐terminated
       strings.  (Do not pass these pointers to ffrreeee(3).)

