VVEERRSSIIOONNSS
       There are two different versions of bbaasseennaammee() ‐ the POSIX version
       described above, and the GNU version, which one gets after

               ##ddeeffiinnee __GGNNUU__SSOOUURRCCEE         /* See feature_test_macros(7) */
               ##iinncclluuddee <<ssttrriinngg..hh>>

       The GNU version never modifies its argument, and returns the empty
       string when _p_a_t_h has a trailing slash, and in particular also when it
       is "/".  There is no GNU version of ddiirrnnaammee().

       With glibc, one gets the POSIX version of bbaasseennaammee() when _<_l_i_b_g_e_n_._h_> is
       included, and the GNU version otherwise.

SSTTAANNDDAARRDDSS
       POSIX.1‐2008.

HHIISSTTOORRYY
       POSIX.1‐2001.

BBUUGGSS
       In the glibc implementation, the POSIX versions of these functions
       modify the _p_a_t_h argument, and segfault when called with a static string
       such as "/usr/".

       Before glibc 2.2.1, the glibc version of ddiirrnnaammee() did not correctly
       handle pathnames with trailing '/' characters, and generated a segfault
       if given a NULL argument.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
       The following code snippet demonstrates the use of bbaasseennaammee() and
       ddiirrnnaammee():
           char *dirc, *basec, *bname, *dname;
           char *path = "/etc/passwd";

           dirc = strdup(path);
           basec = strdup(path);
           dname = dirname(dirc);
           bname = basename(basec);
           printf("dirname=%s, basename=%s\n", dname, bname);

